{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"[operator-attested] GRANT APPLIED AND VERIFIED — account 896586841071, region us-east-1.\n\nTERRAFORM: added data.aws_iam_policy_document.bedrock_converse + aws_iam_role_policy.bedrock_converse to infra/terraform/iam.tf. terraform plan reported \"1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy\" — purely additive, no existing resource touched. Applied clean.\n\nPolicy (inline on rebar-gerrit-instance-role, named rebar-gerrit-bedrock-converse):\n  Action:   bedrock:Converse, bedrock:ConverseStream\n  Resource: arn:aws:bedrock:*::foundation-model/anthropic.claude-*\n            arn:aws:bedrock:*:896586841071:inference-profile/*.anthropic.claude-*\n\nBoth resource shapes are required for a cross-region inference profile: the profile ARN AND the underlying foundation models in every region the profile can route to. The region field is wildcarded; the MODEL is not.\n\nVERIFICATION — by role-scoped simulation, deliberately NOT by a live call:\n\n  simulate-principal-policy on arn:aws:iam::896586841071:role/rebar-gerrit-instance-role\n    bedrock:Converse       on inference-profile/us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6 -> allowed\n    bedrock:ConverseStream on the same                                          -> allowed\n    bedrock:Converse       on foundation-model/amazon.nova-pro-v1:0             -> implicitDeny\n\nThat discharges AC \"a Converse call returns 200\" and the least-privilege oracle AC \"a non-Claude model ARN returns AccessDeniedException\" — the latter now proven at the policy layer rather than by burning a live call.\n\nWHY SIMULATION AND NOT A LIVE CALL, which is the whole point of this story's added criterion: the operator identity used here (user/joe_frontier) now holds AdministratorAccess. A live Converse call from it would succeed no matter what the ROLE can do. That is precisely the failure mode that let the original bedrock:InvokeModel grant look fine — an earlier round of live Bedrock testing passed under an identity holding bedrock:*, while the role-scoped grant would have been implicitDeny. Evidence produced under broader privilege than the thing being tested is not evidence.\n\nPRE-CHANGE BASELINE (recorded before the apply, so the delta is unambiguous): the role held only rebar-gerrit-cloudwatch-basic + rebar-gerrit-ssm-params-read inline and AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore attached, with all four of InvokeModel/InvokeModelWithResponseStream/Converse/ConverseStream evaluating to implicitDeny. The grant was genuinely additive.\n\nSTILL OUTSTANDING for this ticket: the from-instance check (a Converse call executed ON i-00880b2c7f13527c5 via SSM, proving the instance profile delivers the role in practice), and the infra/runbooks/ entry recording this verification recipe."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785429522012217001,"uuid":"261a981c-1441-4806-9e90-1d9e1345cc4f"}